  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14101062/assets/6f445040526c9b72e66dd29b15b32a89ec8b47a4cd2ae17bed009948dbce655d.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- There exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=3$\n- $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(4)=3$ (we have an arrow from $4\\in X$ to $3\\in Y$). So, there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
